WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.480
SQL injection can let attackers change the meaning of database queries and access, modify,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.060
or delete records.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.600
Any application feature that builds database statements from input needs strong query

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:12.700
boundaries.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.560
Command injection is especially severe because it can allow remote command execution

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:23.060
through file processing, diagnostics, administrative utilities, or integration jobs.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.760
LDAP and NoSQL injection follow the same core pattern:

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.500
attacker-controlled input changes the meaning of a query or filter.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:37.720
Identity, directory, search, and API features all require careful input handling.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.660
Input validation, parameterized operations, and output encoding work together.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.320
Validation rejects unexpected values, parameterization separates data from instructions,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.060
and encoding protects data when it is displayed. Use parameterized queries and safe APIs.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.240
Validate input against expected formats and ranges.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.300
Encode output for the correct context and avoid detailed error leakage.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:09.120
Selected CMMC mappings for this topic include SI.L2-3.14.2 - Flaw remediation;

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:15.500
SI.L2-3.14.5 - Protection from malicious code and input abuse.
